{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/reference/architecture.md at exact head 33843691460da55c7c20fdca62db4e825bef0b86 on request/onboarding-identity-core, pushed to origin. It replaces the withdrawn artifact 76b50001 and carries the documentation half of request 2c9bf640 under promise 2999b16a. The predecessor at this path is 1b996100.\n\nWHAT THE PAGE NOW RECORDS. A \"Host identity\" section under layer 4, saying why identity sits at the host binding's seam: an application must not have to invent it, and a host must read it without already holding an application profile, so the vocabulary is fixed there and inherited. It names the three schema families, the anchor, the two axes, the witness authority and its scheme bound, delegation and inheritance, resolution as the authority with nothing gating appends, and judgement against the sequencer's signed timestamp rather than a reader's clock. The layer table gains a host/identity row. The compatibility-axis table now reads \"the exported surface of host and host/identity\" where it read \"the host package's exported surface\".\n\nWHAT THIS REPAIR CHANGED IN THE PAGE. Two corrections answering codex's findings on the withdrawn wave.\n\nThe vouching paragraph no longer describes a self-signed rung as though it were implemented. It says one rung is implemented — witnessed, where a deployment's key says a provider said so — and that the stronger rung, where the identity's own key signs, is deferred along with the providers that would occupy it, Nostr and a published forge signing key. It says why nothing names it: a value the code can never produce reads to a caller as a state to handle and to a reviewer as a rung that was built. The axis stays ordered so adding it later is one more value and the reduction rule the verification axis already follows. This matches the code at this head, where the exported SelfSigned constant is gone and a delegation inherits its endorser's vouching outright.\n\nThe provider-check paragraph now states the rule that keeps a bearer token out of a log, because it is a trust boundary and belongs on the page that records this layer's contract: no byte of provider- or transport-controlled text reaches an error from the check. A refusal reports the numeric status with this program's own phrase for it; a transport failure or an unreadable answer reports fixed words of the package's own. It records that redaction was considered and refused, because it removes only the spelling it goes looking for and the party echoing the credential chooses the spelling.\n\nCITATIONS AND GATE. The page's rests_on carries the live host artifact 969a4e7a among its bases. internal/docset, the citation gate, passes at this exact head under both go test -count=1 ./... and go test -race -count=1 ./..., so every citation on the page resolves against the log as it stands. gofmt, go build ./... and go vet ./... are also clean at this head.\n\nSCOPE. docs/reference/architecture.md is the only file under docs that this head touches. No other page was edited, and no claim here describes behaviour outside host/identity.","body":{"commit":"33843691460da55c7c20fdca62db4e825bef0b86","path":"docs"}}